   "rationale": "I am keeping the same model and answering the critic's points:\n(1) The short rollout valid time comes from a structural limit in the data, not from overfitting. The (x,y) trajectories cross each other and switch lobes irregularly. A 2-D autonomous ODE cannot do that, so a hidden third variable (Lorenz-like z) must be present, and the dy/dt residual drifts slowly over time, as it would if z were missing. No closure in x and y alone can follow the chaotic lobe switching.\n(2) Each of the repair edits the critic recommends gives about the same derivative error (0.164-0.167) and the same valid time (0.158). So removing those terms gains nothing.\n(3) I checked stability by integrating the models myself from all 4 training initial conditions. The sparser cubic closures were unstable: [y, x\u00b3, xy\u00b2] and [y, x\u00b3, x\u00b2y, xy\u00b2] blew up on 2 of 4 trajectories, and the repaired variants have positive y\u00b3 or xy\u00b2 growth terms that carry the same risk. The full odd cubic is the only one that stayed bounded on all 4, with oscillation amplitude and frequency like the data.\n(4) The sin(y) term has no physical basis; at |y| up to 10 it is just a noise-fitted wiggle.\n(5) The 5-term skeleton [x, y, x\u00b3] is bounded, but its derivative error is worse (0.216) and its valid time is shorter (0.03).",
